# armbfxwidth32 (commit c0c7f55) -- REVIEW READING: what does the shipped diff claim, and what could escape it?

This is an ANALYSIS request about a diff that has ALREADY SHIPPED. Please do not write, propose or
sketch any code, patch or guest sequence. Prose only.

## THE TREE
`C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\`. Ignore any other `src/` under the project root.

## SCOPE -- read EXACTLY these
    the diff below (inlined), and for context ONLY the three instruction handlers it touches in
    src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c (ubfx, sbfx, bfi) and the row list of regress/arm_bfx_width_probe.py.
    The ARM manual is LOCAL: `C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\_scratchpad\ddi0406.txt` -- A8.8.20 (BFI)
    at line 18066 and the UBFX/SBFX sections nearby. Cite it by line if you use it.

## SETTLED
`ubfx/sbfx/bfi` built their mask with `1 << width` where the decoder lets width reach 32, and bfi's
width can go negative -- three UB regions across eight sites. `#510` fixed UBFX/SBFX (widthm1
encoding, never negative); BFI (MSB encoding, width in [-30, 32]) is the separate row `armbfiwidth`.
Witness artifacts: `regress/artifacts/armbfxwidth32/witness_HEAD_20260907.raw`,
`witness_FIXED_20260907.raw`, `witness_FIXED12_20260907.raw`, and one mutant `M_nosext.raw`.
The flagship review stage was HELD for the 2026-09-09 adjudication, now being done.

## THE QUESTIONS -- answer extensionally
**Q1.** From the diff: which PREDICATES does the fix ADD or CHANGE at each of the sites it touches,
and which existing predicates dominate them up to the enclosing handler boundary?
**Q2.** For each, the smallest WEAKENING edit that still reads like the same check -- and whether the
ONE preserved mutant (`M_nosext`) is that edit. Every predicate with no measured mutant is an
unmeasured cell; name each.
**Q3.** THE WIDTH-32 QUESTION: at width 32 the manual says UBFX copies the whole field. Under the
fix, what does width 32 produce, and is there a row that pins it? Under the OLD code, what did it
produce on a count-masked host shift? If those differ, the fix is a BEHAVIOUR change, not only a
definedness repair -- say whether the diff's own comments say so.
**Q4.** `#510`'s own finding: `1 << 31` and `1U << 31` give the same mask after the `- 1`, so NO
guest-value row can catch a `1U` -> `1` regression. Does the shipped detector CLAIM to cover that,
anywhere in its header? Quote it if so.

VERDICT LINE FIRST, then the body. If the diff is not what the premises describe, say so first.

